  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9b6579ee",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In the raw file legitimate sites are the majority (about 56%). The duplicates are mostly legitimate rows, so after\n",
    "removing them the classes are close to balanced and **phishing becomes the slight majority**. The data is not\n",
    "seriously imbalanced either way, so stratified splitting is enough and no resampling is needed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "133c9065",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Cleaning decisions\n",
    "\n",
    "| Issue | Decision | Reason |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Exact duplicate rows | **Drop before splitting** | Prevents identical rows appearing in both train and test (leakage, shown in 3.4). |\n",
    "| Conflicting labels | **Keep** | They are real, ambiguous cases; removing them would make the task look easier than it is. Reported as a limitation. |\n",
    "| Missing values / invalid codes | None found | No action needed. |\n",
    "| Target coding | Recode to `is_phishing` (1 = phishing, 0 = legitimate) | Makes phishing the *positive class*, so recall/precision/F1 refer to catching phishing. |\n",
    "\n",
    "*Trade-off:* dropping duplicates means the models are evaluated on **distinct feature patterns** rather than on the\n",
    "original frequency of each pattern. I think this is the more honest test of generalisation, but it is a decision I\n",
    "will discuss as a limitation in the report."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "5ce0eeb9",
   "metadata": {
