   "source": [
    "## 6. Preprocessing pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "The features are codes (-1/0/1), not measured quantities. I **one-hot encode** them, so a model does not assume that\n",
    "the gap between \"phishing-like\" and \"suspicious\" is the same as between \"suspicious\" and \"legitimate-like\".\n",
    "This matters most for distance- and weight-based models (k-NN, logistic regression); tree models would work on the\n",
    "raw codes too. No scaling is needed after one-hot encoding because every column is 0/1.\n",
    "`handle_unknown=\"ignore\"` means a code never seen during training does not crash the pipeline.\n",
    "\n",
    "The encoder is defined once here and reused, inside a pipeline, for every model in Milestone 3, so all models get the\n",
    "same inputs and the comparison is fair."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "id": "5ceb7d6f",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-29T13:08:37.583268Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-29T13:08:37.583159Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-29T13:08:37.591725Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-29T13:08:37.591311Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "30 coded features -> 68 one-hot columns\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "def make_preprocessor():\n",
    "    return OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\", sparse_output=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Demonstration only: fit on the training data to show the output size\n",
    "demo = make_preprocessor().fit(X_train)\n",
    "print(f\"{X_train.shape[1]} coded features -> {len(demo.get_feature_names_out())} one-hot columns\")"
   ]
  },
